{:paths ["src"]

 ;; The oldest jolt glitter runs on, declared so a runtime below it refuses
 ;; to load rather than run wrong (jolt #804). glitter is a library, and a
 ;; library is the natural declarer here: it knows what its bindings need
 ;; and the app pulling it in does not.
 ;;
 ;; 0.7.24 is the release that made (t/today) answer the machine's zone
 ;; instead of UTC: the TZ-restore fix and the memoization that makes it
 ;; affordable. Both are absent from v0.7.23. Without them
 ;; examples/glitter/flights.clj opens its departure field on YESTERDAY for
 ;; the first 10 hours of every AEST day, silently. That is the exact shape
 ;; of failure this key turns into a message. See docs/guide/nexus.md.
 ;;
 ;; This does NOT fire today, and it is worth being honest about why. The key
 ;; is only honoured by a jolt that reads it, and the runtime that reads it
 ;; (#804) first shipped in v0.8.0, far above this floor. So every jolt that
 ;; can enforce the floor already clears it, and every jolt old enough to have
 ;; the date bug ignores the key the way it ignores any key it does not know.
 ;; Until v0.8.0 shipped on 2026-09-01 the sharper form was "no released jolt
 ;; reads the key at all"; that is no longer true, and the conclusion is
 ;; unchanged. The declaration is forward protection, in place for the next
 ;; break, which is the whole reason upstream shipped the key before needing
 ;; it rather than after.
 ;;
 ;; Think twice before RAISING this, because a floor is enforced on consumers,
 ;; not just here. A project that declares no floor of its own still gets
 ;; refused at resolve-project when a dependency declares one it cannot meet,
 ;; and the message names the dependency (:jolt/required-by). Measured against
 ;; a :local/root dep declaring "0.9.0": the consumer is stopped before any of
 ;; its code runs. So whatever glitter declares becomes a hard stop for
 ;; glitter-gl, glitter-uikit and anyone else downstream, over a constraint
 ;; they never chose. Raise it only for something glitter genuinely needs, and
 ;; only to a version that is actually released.
 ;;
 ;; Deliberately NOT raised to 0.7.29 for jolt #802's ffi/write argument-order
 ;; break: glitter has no ffi/write call site and no [:array ...] layout
 ;; descriptor, so that break cannot reach it. Raising the floor for a change
 ;; glitter does not use would refuse working runtimes for nothing, and a
 ;; library's floor propagates to every consumer.
 ;;
 ;; And a correction worth keeping, because jolt's own changelog invites the
 ;; opposite conclusion by using ffi/write as this key's motivating example:
 ;; NO floor value protects an ffi/write consumer. Not "0.7.29", not "0.8.0",
 ;; none. The populations are disjoint by construction, and that is provable
 ;; rather than merely likely: #804 (which added the code that READS this key)
 ;; has #802's merge commit as its SOLE parent, ahead by exactly one, so no
 ;; jolt exists that reads the key and still carries the old write order. A
 ;; runtime with the old order predates the reader and skips the key. Measured
 ;; elsewhere: released v0.7.29 against a project declaring "0.8.0" ran 19 of
 ;; 37 tests, dropped two namespaces silently, and exited clean. The key
 ;; guards breaks from #804 FORWARD, and nothing before it.
 :jolt/min-version "0.7.24"

 ;; jolt-lang/time provides the java.time.* surface as a pure-Clojure
 ;; host shim, with juxt/tick (pulled transitively via jolt-lang/time's
 ;; own deps.edn) as the idiomatic date/time API on top — used by
 ;; examples/glitter/flights.clj for date parsing/formatting/comparison.
 ;; jolt-lang/logging ports clojure.tools.logging — used by
 ;; glitter.nexus consumers as the :nexus/on-error handler. Both pinned
 ;; SHAs are the known-good versions, verified live under real Jolt (not
 ;; just JVM Clojure) as of when they were pinned — re-verify live
 ;; before bumping either.
 ;;
 ;; The time SHA is a FLOOR, not just a snapshot, and it now sits two commits
 ;; PAST the v0.0.7 tag rather than on it. Two separate reasons, both of which
 ;; break silently if the pin moves back:
 ;;
 ;; v0.0.7 itself is what made (t/today) answer the machine's zone instead of
 ;; UTC. Pinning older puts flights.clj's departure field back on the UTC date.
 ;;
 ;; 33c5e98 is jolt-lang/time#11, which declares the java.time classes the
 ;; library provides (jolt #813 / RFC 0014). Without it, a jolt carrying #813
 ;; cannot resolve java.time.ZoneId at all: `jolt flights` dies, and
 ;; glitter.nexus.action-log-test is silently dropped from the suite, 29 tests
 ;; down to 26 with a clean exit. It is untagged because no release carries it
 ;; yet, so this pin is ahead of v0.0.7 deliberately. Move it to the tag once
 ;; one ships. See docs/guide/nexus.md.
 ;;
 ;; The two use different Maven-coordinate conventions on purpose:
 ;; io.github.jolt-lang/time is a normal git-coordinate dep, but
 ;; org.clojure/tools.logging deliberately SHADOWS the real upstream
 ;; org.clojure/tools.logging coordinate — any transitive dependency
 ;; that pulls in the real clojure.tools.logging gets this jolt-lang
 ;; shim instead. Not a coordinate-naming inconsistency to "fix."
 :deps {weavejester/hiccup
        {:git/url "https://github.com/weavejester/hiccup"
         :git/sha "b6b12097ba973141fe87c1b4d6a27ddcce5d5929"}
        io.github.jolt-lang/time
        {:git/url "https://github.com/jolt-lang/time"
         :git/sha "33c5e9831c046cfcbe68bc0c9c33e92568eaebb1"}
        org.clojure/tools.logging
        {:git/url "https://github.com/jolt-lang/logging"
         :git/sha "ec6747e55583118d881faf4ab41e68d7e587ce4c"}}

 ;; GTK4 and its GLib dependencies, bound natively via jolt.ffi. Identical
 ;; declarations to glimmer's own deps.edn (jolt-lang/glimmer) — glitter
 ;; targets the same native libraries, just a different reconciler on top.
 :jolt/native [{:name "glib-2.0"
                :darwin ["/opt/homebrew/lib/libglib-2.0.0.dylib"
                         "/usr/local/lib/libglib-2.0.0.dylib"
                         "libglib-2.0.0.dylib"
                         "libglib-2.0.dylib"]
                :linux  ["libglib-2.0.so.0"
                         "libglib-2.0.so"]}
               {:name "gobject-2.0"
                :darwin ["/opt/homebrew/lib/libgobject-2.0.0.dylib"
                         "/usr/local/lib/libgobject-2.0.0.dylib"
                         "libgobject-2.0.0.dylib"
                         "libgobject-2.0.dylib"]
                :linux  ["libgobject-2.0.so.0"
                         "libgobject-2.0.so"]}
               {:name "gio-2.0"
                :darwin ["/opt/homebrew/lib/libgio-2.0.0.dylib"
                         "/usr/local/lib/libgio-2.0.0.dylib"
                         "libgio-2.0.0.dylib"
                         "libgio-2.0.dylib"]
                :linux  ["libgio-2.0.so.0"
                         "libgio-2.0.so"]}
               {:name "gtk-4"
                :darwin ["/opt/homebrew/lib/libgtk-4.1.dylib"
                         "/usr/local/lib/libgtk-4.1.dylib"
                         "libgtk-4.1.dylib"
                         "libgtk-4.dylib"]
                :linux  ["libgtk-4.so.1"
                         "libgtk-4.so"]}]

